vine 1.2.0
prefix: bench
title: machine-agnostic CI benchmark baselines
---
[root] Establish machine-agnostic CI benchmark baselines (reviewing)
The set-upsert implementation is committed. This graph plans the next
validation instrument: unit benchmarks that report comparable work and
resource measures, not elapsed time on one machine.
Each node states the measurement question it owns, what its absence would cost,
and the evidence that would settle it. Sibling nodes own disjoint metric or
lifecycle boundaries and relate only through dependency edges.
The benchmark system must preserve the repository principles: measure before
judging, report data rather than rankings, keep bad states out of the artifact,
and keep benchmark machinery outside the supported product surface.
The accepted Grimoire source is now a merged but not-yet-published package;
the source node records the immutable revision and policy needed before bridge
implementation.
-> checkpoint
@guidance text/markdown docs/product-planning/architecture.md
@guidance text/markdown docs/product-planning/lifecycle.md
@guidance text/markdown README.md
@guidance text/yaml .github/workflows/ci.yml
---
[contract] Define the benchmark contract (complete)
Fix what a CI baseline is allowed to claim and how an update is reviewed.
Serves: every workload and metric has one interpretation.
Absent: a number can look precise while measuring a machine, a cache, or an
unrecorded change in the input.
Known by: a versioned artifact schema, units, identity fields, gate rules, and
a baseline-update rule that a reviewer can apply without guessing.
Do: distinguish exact work counts, logical resource budgets, and diagnostic
environment observations; exclude elapsed time from regression gates; bind a
measurement to code/model/workload revisions; define how intentional changes
replace a baseline.
Accept: one contract rejects machine-specific performance claims and leaves no
semantic choice for workload, metric, harness, or CI nodes.
> The benchmark is a validation instrument, not a speed ranking. A gated field must be derivable from pinned code/model/workload inputs and expressed in a stable unit; elapsed time, CPU frequency, utilization, scheduler behavior, raw RSS, and hardware performance counters are diagnostic only.
> The artifact identity needs schema version, workload id and input fingerprint, model name and pinned revision, metric name/unit/basis, and collection boundary. Code revision and runner metadata may be retained for provenance but are not comparison keys or pass/fail inputs.
> Exact equality is the default for deterministic counts and bytes. A budget or tolerance is allowed only when the owning metric node demonstrates an irreducible source of variation and records it; no blanket percentage threshold is introduced.
> A baseline update is a deliberate data change reviewed with the implementation or model/workload change that explains it. A schema, model, or workload identity mismatch fails loudly instead of silently regenerating the reference.
> The benchmark runner and artifacts remain validation/build machinery, not supported scry or MCP API. The current CI workflow is the integration target, but no CI edit or benchmark value is part of this planning node.
> Grimoire PR #1 is merged on `main` at `bd9920bc1ae79d40383fedcacea3adb87fd98109`; no release tag or crates.io publication is available yet, so the benchmark source must be pinned independently of the registry.
---
[workloads] Fix deterministic unit workloads (complete)
Choose deterministic unit workloads and fixed tensor shapes whose measures will form the Grimoire-backed baseline matrix.
Serves: CI compares the same static description, explicit axes, cost reports, and runtime-owned resource work after an implementation update.
Absent: changes in corpus size, tensor shape, graph topology, model bytes, randomness, network state, or filesystem paths are confused with implementation changes.
Known by: named workloads with fixed bytes, token targets, padded batch shapes, static Grimoire graph identity, set sizes, report sizes, model revision, and expected structural outputs.
Do: cover the owned library boundaries that matter to update validation: origin normalization, slicing, passage embedding, add orchestration, record encoding/decoding, search over a fixed corpus, and handle/report mapping where useful. Use in-memory or reserved non-network material, fixed seeds, and no current time or absolute temporary paths in measured identities. For model calls, record rectangular ONNX batch size B and padded sequence length S and preserve the generated Grimoire description rather than reducing it to a word-count table.
Accept: each case has a stable workload id and input fingerprint; its token, chunk, member, output, Grimoire element/group, and tensor-shape cardinalities are explicit; no case requires network or an ambient corpus.
-> contract
-> grimoire
-> operations
> A workload's model component is the canonical serialized Grimoire description plus graph/config hashes and explicit axis bindings. Structural group membership may be counted for sanity, but FLOPs are obtained by evaluating cost expressions over addressed elements and shapes.
> Variable-length model calls are excluded from gated static cost cases because fastembed pads each batch to a rectangular [B,S] input. B and S are workload identity, and S is the padded encoded length rather than the sum of source token lengths.
> The first bridge fixture uses the pinned BGE graph and should retain at least one opaque operation such as Erf or Sqrt and one shape-aware MatMul. A graph topology or unsupported-shape change fails identity/cost validation rather than being absorbed into a tolerance.
---
[metrics] Define the machine-agnostic metric vocabulary (complete)
Define the machine-agnostic metric vocabulary, Grimoire static representations, cost projections, and runtime-owned resource units in which derived values are compared.
Serves: baseline diffs compare like with like.
Absent: RAM, FLOPs, bytes, and counts collapse into an ambiguous performance score.
Known by: a metric table with exactness, unit, collection boundary, and whether the value gates CI or is diagnostic only.
Do: distinguish the canonical serialized Grimoire description from runtime resource observations and from Grimoire CostModel-derived metric vectors; define explicit operation/group/cardinality facts, tensor extents, model invocation counts, nominal cost projections, requested allocation/live-byte measures, source and record byte counts, transaction counts, and artifact cardinalities. Keep computed FLOPs distinct from sourced measurement values, hardware instructions, and elapsed time.
Accept: every emitted field has one owner, one Grimoire/static or runtime basis, one cost-model version where derived, and one comparison rule; no gated metric depends on CPU frequency, scheduler behavior, OS RSS policy, or runner wall-clock speed.
-> contract
-> grimoire
-> operations
> Metric lineage is now `Grimoire description -> explicit shape/axis bindings -> Grimoire CostModel/CostReport projection` for static work, and `owned runtime boundary -> private resource counter` for bytes/transactions/live bytes. These are different evidence paths and remain separately named in the artifact.
> A derived metric may be recomputed from the serialized description and cost expression assignments. The static description is the auditable source for operation words; no runtime operation-occurrence event is needed for FLOP validation.
---
[memory] Resolve the stable memory measure (complete)
Choose what CI means by RAM usage without pretending RSS is portable.
Serves: memory regressions have a reproducible baseline.
Absent: a memory number is either machine noise or silently omits the model and
native runtime allocations.
Known by: a measured definition of peak logical bytes and/or requested
allocation high-water, its included ownership, and an explicit treatment of
native model memory and raw RSS.
Do: test the allocator and ownership instrumentation against fixed workloads;
separate scry-owned data structures from pinned model assets and native runtime
state; state which value is a CI gate and which values are diagnostic.
Accept: the chosen gate is machine-agnostic under the stated workload, can be
collected without a public API change, and cannot be mistaken for process RSS.
-> metrics
-> workloads
> Memory observations follow the operation vocabulary but are not cost weights: operation events may delimit owned-live-byte measurement regions, while raw RSS and native runtime residency remain diagnostic. No operation word receives a fabricated RAM price in the gated baseline.
---
[compute] Resolve the stable compute measure (complete)
Compile and validate the pinned raw model graph as a Grimoire static description with explicit shapes and axes.
Serves: the static representation has verified topology and shape rules before Grimoire cost projections are evaluated.
Absent: a FLOP expression can look exact while silently omitting a node, counting a fused runtime graph, or using the wrong padded input shape.
Known by: graph hash, canonical Grimoire serialization, element/group topology, operator-word facts, shape propagation rules, and a repeatable analyzer that rejects topology or unsupported-shape changes.
Do: parse the pinned ONNX graph offline; generate the Grimoire description and operation groups; verify weighted and attention MatMul shapes for `(B,S)`; keep raw graph analysis, Grimoire validation, cost projection, and runtime optimization as separate boundaries.
Accept: the generated description round-trips canonically, reproduces the pinned structural fixture, records model/config identity, and supplies explicit addressed shapes/axes to cost-model-v1 without loading or timing the runtime graph.
-> grimoire
-> metrics
-> operations
-> workloads
> The earlier raw node census remains a structural acceptance fixture only. It is not an observed FLOP measurement and is not the primary artifact layer; the canonical static artifact is the validated Grimoire description.
> The generated description must distinguish model graph topology from runtime ORT Level3 optimization. ORT's optimized graph and provider execution are diagnostics and cannot replace the raw Grimoire input.
> The bridge must fail if a model node lacks an address, operator identity, required port/connection representation, or a shape needed by a selected cost projection. It must not silently infer missing extents from operator names.
---
[io] Count deterministic data movement (complete)
Measure source, corpus, and serialization work without measuring device speed.
Serves: storage and ingestion regressions have a machine-independent signal.
Absent: a change can move more bytes, open more transactions, or refetch more
material while a time benchmark happens to stay flat.
Known by: exact counts for input bytes, output/record bytes, spans,
embeddings, transactions, and fetch/member attempts at each owned boundary.
Do: identify counters that can be collected from private seams and fixed
fixtures; separate logical bytes from filesystem allocation and network
transfer; include empty, singleton, and multi-member set cases.
Accept: counters are deterministic for one workload and do not require an
ambient filesystem, network service, or OS-specific allocation report.
-> metrics
-> workloads
> Planning decision complete: logical byte and operation counters are the gate. Filesystem allocation, network transfer rate, cache behavior, device latency, and other machine-specific observations are excluded from pass/fail.
---
[instrumentation] Build private runtime resource seams (complete)
Implement private runtime resource measurement seams that remain separate from Grimoire static cost analysis.
Serves: workloads emit owned byte, transaction, and live-resource measures at the boundary that owns them.
Absent: benchmark code duplicates production logic or mistakes runtime observations for static graph cost.
Known by: focused unit tests prove counters reset, compose across one workload, and cannot alter ordinary product behavior when benchmarks are disabled.
Do: place private sinks/test-only hooks beside fetch, slice, embed, add, store, and MCP boundaries only for runtime-owned logical bytes, transactions, member/report cardinalities, and owned-live-byte regions; do not emit FLOP occurrence counters or a second operation vocabulary; leave static operation identity and nominal cost to the Grimoire bridge.
Accept: normal builds have no benchmark state or public exports, each runtime counter has one owner and test-only collection path, and no runtime observation is used to derive gated FLOPs.
-> io
-> memory
-> workloads
> The prior runtime operation-word counter design is superseded. Runtime seams collect only observations that belong to runtime boundaries: logical bytes, transactions, report/member cardinalities, and owned-live-byte regions.
> Static operator identity, operation-family grouping, shape expansion, and nominal FLOP computation belong entirely to the Grimoire description/CostModel path. Instrumentation must not duplicate or reconcile that static graph.
> Implemented as `scry`'s `benchmark-instrumentation` feature: normal builds compile no collector state, while benchmark builds collect private boundary observations and expose only `BenchmarkCollector`/`BenchmarkSnapshot` to the benchmark target.
> Collector tests prove fresh reset, nested restoration, counter ownership, and visible overflow. The v1 run observed source bytes, spans, model calls/vectors, redb transactions, scan/result cardinalities, add outcomes, provenance lookups, and the high-water mark of selected logical owned buffers.
---
[harness] Emit versioned benchmark artifacts (complete)
Emit versioned benchmark artifacts containing the canonical Grimoire description and Grimoire-derived cost reports, plus separately labeled runtime resource data.
Serves: CI can collect and parse the same static cost analysis and owned resource measures on every update.
Absent: baselines live in terminal prose, depend on benchmark-framework timing, or lose the graph facts behind a scalar.
Known by: one deterministic JSON/JSONL artifact with Grimoire/schema/model/graph/workload identity, canonical description, explicit axis bindings, operation/group facts, cost-model version, derived reports, and no timestamps or machine-specific values in gated fields.
Do: use a benchmark-side runner that can reach the private runtime seams where needed; serialize the validated Grimoire description as the static source artifact; evaluate named Grimoire CostModels; emit runtime counters separately; do not make elapsed time the primary output. Make malformed, incomplete, unknown-word, unsupported-shape, invalid-Grimoire, and identity-mismatched artifacts fail loudly.
Accept: a clean checkout can emit an artifact offline after required assets are present, the static description and cost reports round-trip/validate, derived values are recomputable, and the runner has no public product API or hidden ambient corpus state.
-> contract
-> cost-model
-> grimoire
-> grimoire-bridge
-> instrumentation
-> metrics
-> operations
-> workloads
> The artifact's primary source layer is a canonical Grimoire description or serialized cut, not an operation-counter table. It carries the Grimoire core/projection/schema versions and exact source revision used by the bridge.
> Derived reports retain the expression/model identity and named unit. If the current Grimoire text grammar cannot serialize symbolic cost expressions, the runner stores the expression assignments in a separate versioned benchmark artifact section rather than inventing projection syntax.
> The artifact records the exact Grimoire Git URL and revision while the benchmark source is Git-based; moving to a published package is a reviewed identity change, not an automatic dependency refresh.
> Implemented as `benchmarks/baseline-v1.json`, emitted by `scry-benchmarks` with canonical JSON ordering. `--check` regenerates in memory and reports the first differing line without overwriting the baseline.
---
[baseline] Establish committed baselines and update discipline (reviewing)
Establish committed Grimoire static-description and cost-report baselines with update discipline.
Serves: future updates have a concrete validation target for graph structure, shapes, assumptions, and runtime-owned resources.
Absent: every update redefines its own reference and a changed graph or weighting disappears into a moving scalar expectation.
Known by: a committed baseline artifact with canonical Grimoire description/cut, graph and workload identity, explicit axes, named cost-model reports, exact or budget comparison rules, and separately labeled diagnostics.
Do: collect values from the fixed workload matrix, review Grimoire addresses/groups, shapes, operator facts, units, graph/model/workload/cost-model versions, classify any non-exact runtime metric, and store only reproducible fields. Make baseline changes visible as data diffs and require the implementation, model, graph, or cost-model change that explains them.
Accept: the baseline contains no wall-clock or runner-specific gate, can be recreated from pinned inputs, and fails loudly when the Grimoire description, schema, operation representation, cost model, graph, or model identity changes unexpectedly.
-> cost-model
-> grimoire-bridge
-> harness
-> operations
> Compare canonical Grimoire structure, operator/group membership, and shape rows before comparing CostReports. A changed `Erf` group or `MatMul` shape is meaningful even if a later cost-model projection happens to preserve a total.
> A Grimoire source revision or schema change is an identity change requiring explicit review. It is not a reason to rewrite the baseline automatically or compare unlike descriptions.
> The first baseline artifact is generated at `benchmarks/baseline-v1.json` and records exact structural/static fields plus deterministic runtime observations. It remains in review until the artifact and CI change are committed together.
> Generated v1 data: graph hash `828e1496d7fabb79cfa4dcd84fa38625c0d3d21da474a00f08db0f559940cf35`, canonical description hash `6718baf31b789524c065790e2d2ad9d64b4f96c9ac0889fd66acc9c4705fe31b`, 1,244 ONNX nodes, 1,662 connections, 688,914,432 MACs, and 1,377,828,864 FMA FLOPs at `(B,S)=(1,32)`.
> The same artifact records 10 sliced spans, 3 embedding calls, 3 writes, 4 reads, 3 add members with 2 upserts and 1 refusal, 3 search hits, 2 neighbours, 2 provenance lookups, and a 13,343-byte `owned_logical_bytes_high_water`. `--check` passes byte-for-byte after regeneration.
