[operations] Represent operation words in Grimoire (complete)
Represent operation identity and structural grouping through the Grimoire static description rather than a hand-maintained runtime occurrence counter.
Serves: the operation vocabulary is addressable, serializable, and auditable before cost is applied.
Absent: a local counter table can hide graph topology changes and cannot preserve per-node shape or provenance.
Known by: every model operation has one addressed structural element, an explicit operator word, a shape basis where known, and a group relation for aggregation.
Do: use Grimoire addressed blocks and groups for operation instances and families; carry raw operator identity, domain/opset/dtype, node address, tensor ports, and shape/axis facts; derive family cardinalities from group membership and let unsupported words fail at the cost boundary.
Accept: no FLOP value is collected by runtime observation, no operation is silently placed in `other`, and canonical Grimoire serialization preserves the complete operation structure.
-> contract
-> grimoire
> The earlier hand-authored `operation-vocabulary-v1` occurrence census is superseded as the source of truth. The operation words now live in the generated Grimoire description as structural operator values and addressed group membership; any aggregate count is a derived structural query.
> The measured 19-word ONNX census remains a fixture expectation for the first bridge, not a runtime metric: Add 172, Cast 1, Concat 48, Constant 285, Div 49, Erf 12, Gather 100, MatMul 96, Mul 50, Pow 25, ReduceMean 50, Reshape 48, Shape 97, Slice 1, Softmax 12, Sqrt 25, Sub 26, Transpose 48, Unsqueeze 99. The generated description must reproduce these structural facts or fail.
> A model node address is structural identity within one generated description; operator word is semantic grouping data; shape basis is the input to cost algebra. Exporter node names may be diagnostic and must not be the only identity source.
> The bridge may preserve an operator that has no cost row, but a gated cost projection must reject an unpriced/unsupported word rather than silently dropping it or assigning it to an opaque scalar bucket.
---
[cost-model] Apply Grimoire cost algebra (complete)
Apply Grimoire's checked symbolic cost algebra to the validated static operation description.
Serves: FLOPs and other nominal work values are computed from explicit addressed elements, groups, axes, and shape expressions.
Absent: arbitrary per-occurrence weights and opaque scalar totals hide which graph and shape facts produced a value.
Known by: named Grimoire CostExpression assignments, explicit axes, CostModel evaluations, CostReport group totals, and fail-visible handling of missing prices, unsupported words, and overflow.
Do: use Grimoire constant/axis/sum/product expressions over generated operation blocks; assign per-node expressions and aggregate operation-family/group totals; run one named CostModel per derived unit where the current Grimoire API is scalar; retain opaque operations as counts without invented FLOP prices.
Accept: every gated derived value is recomputable from the serialized Grimoire description and named cost model; shape-aware MatMul produces MAC/FMA_FLOP projections; Erf/Sqrt/Softmax remain explicit unpriced operation facts; no runtime observation or hardware counter enters the FLOP result.
-> contract
-> grimoire
-> metrics
-> operations
> The assumption matrix is now authored as Grimoire CostExpression assignments, not a Scry-side occurrence multiplier table. `CostExpression::axis`, `sum`, and `product` provide checked symbolic arithmetic over explicit positive axis bindings; `CostReport::group_total` supplies structural aggregation and rejects missing/unpriced leaves, duplicate members, cycles, unknown elements, and overflow.
> For the pinned transformer, the first named projection remains `MatMul(B,M,K,N) -> MACs = B*M*K*N` and `FMA_FLOPs = 2*B*M*K*N`; the verified aggregate for rectangular `(B,S)` is `B * (42,467,328*S + 18,432*S^2)` FMA_FLOPs. The formula is a consequence of generated addressed nodes and expressions, not a hard-coded runtime observation.
> Use separate named reports for `macs`, `fma_flops`, scalar element operations, logical bytes, or other units until Grimoire has a demonstrated need for a vector-cost API. A scalar weighted score is not introduced.
> Grimoire's cost layer intentionally leaves FLOP, byte, sparsity, precision, and hardware conventions caller-authored. Scry's cost-model-v1 names only the projections it can defend and records every other operation word without assigning it a fake price.
> The first implementation must not try to derive CostExpression assignments from a runtime trace. It builds them from the validated static Grimoire description and explicit shape/axis inputs; a changed source graph or expression row is visible in the artifact.
---
[grimoire] Adopt Grimoire as the static cost boundary (complete)
Choose how Grimoire supplies the canonical static representation for benchmark cost analysis.
Serves: FLOPs and other symbolic work are computed from an addressable validated graph rather than observed through brittle runtime occurrence counters.
Absent: a hand-maintained operation enum or counter map can drift from the model graph while still producing plausible totals.
Known by: a boundary contract separating Grimoire descriptions, schemas, projections, sourced measurements, and runtime observations.
Do: use Grimoire's description/core graph as the static representation; map model nodes to addressed blocks, tensor values to ports and connections, and operation families to addressed groups; use explicit shape/axis facts and host-side cost algebra; keep the product library and MCP surface independent of benchmark machinery.
Accept: the boundary preserves graph identity, operator identity, shape extents, group membership, canonical serialization, and fail-visible validation without treating computed cost as a sourced measurement or adding runtime semantics.
-> contract
> Deep review of Grimoire at clean commit 7cb951ab8e75ae1b33555a0ec3bf2de7f1a85e39: its static description has addressed blocks, ports, connections, groups, layers, schemas, canonical serialization, and validated cuts; its prototype CostExpression/CostModel/CostReport provides checked constant/axis/sum/product arithmetic and addressed group aggregation. Grimoire's full 130-test executable suite and strict clippy pass at this revision.
> Grimoire is the representation and symbolic-analysis boundary, not a runtime observer. An ONNX node becomes an addressed block; inputs and outputs become ports; tensor flow becomes connections; operation-family groups are structural groups. Group cardinality is derived from the static description, not measured from execution.
> Use the existing architecture/1 operator text and shapes/1 port values initially rather than adding a new Grimoire core element kind. A dedicated operation schema is a later extension only if a concrete fixture proves free operator text cannot carry the required validation; unknown raw operator names remain visible and unsupported cost projections fail loudly.
> Do not use Grimoire measurement/1 for computed FLOPs. That schema is for sourced literal observations with value, unit, and source; symbolic cost belongs to host-side cost algebra. Raw RSS, actual runtime FLOPs, and optimized-graph execution remain separate diagnostics.
> Grimoire PR #1 is merged and release-ready at source revision `bd9920bc1ae79d40383fedcacea3adb87fd98109`, with package version `1.0.0`; it has no release tag and is not published to crates.io. Scry must not depend on `../grimoire` in CI or in the product library; the benchmark-only source node now owns the immutable Git pin and cargo-deny policy.
> Current CostModel returns one checked u64 report per model. The first bridge may run separate named models/reports for MACs, FMA_FLOPs, bytes, or other projections; a vector-cost extension is deferred until repeated use proves necessary.
---
[grimoire-source] Establish a reproducible Grimoire source (complete)
Choose how the benchmark bridge obtains Grimoire in a clean checkout and CI.
Serves: the static analysis can be reproduced outside the developer's sibling-repository layout.
Absent: a local `../grimoire` path works only on one machine, while an unpinned Git dependency can change the cost semantics underneath a baseline.
Known by: a source URI, immutable revision or released version, package/license metadata, cargo-deny policy, and a clean-checkout resolution check.
Do: add the benchmark-only dependency on the verified Grimoire Git revision, record the exact URL and revision in `Cargo.lock` and benchmark identity, allow only that repository in `deny.toml`, and keep Grimoire out of the scry runtime product dependencies.
Accept: a fresh checkout resolves the selected package with `cargo metadata --locked` and cargo-deny without a sibling checkout or registry ambiguity; the exact Grimoire revision, package version, and license boundary are recorded; and no unpublished or personal local path is required.
-> grimoire
> Audit result at Grimoire commit 7cb951ab8e75ae1b33555a0ec3bf2de7f1a85e39: the current tracked tree contains no email addresses, local-user paths, secret-like values, or sensitive-looking filenames. The ignored local scry.redb is 269 MB and target/ is ignored; neither is tracked.
> The earlier audit identified `.vscode/mcp.json` as a sibling-checkout publication hazard; merged PR #1 removed it and added the package README, license, and archive checks. A release tag and crates.io publication remain absent.
> The reachable Git history uses the author identity Autumn Wyborny <autumnwyborny@witchfolio.com> on its commits. This is a human publication gate: keep it if the user accepts that public history identity, or rewrite history with explicit approval before pushing. No history rewrite or push is performed here.
> The reviewed PR head passed repository tests, strict Clippy, package creation, and the complete extracted-package test suite. The merged commit is stable enough to pin immutably while publication remains the cleaner long-term boundary.
> The first source is `grimoire = { git = "https://github.com/goldenwitch/grimoire.git", rev = "bd9920bc1ae79d40383fedcacea3adb87fd98109", package = "grimoire" }` in benchmark-only tooling.
> A clean scratch Cargo manifest resolved that revision as package `grimoire` version `1.0.0`; crates.io currently exposes an unrelated historical `grimoire` `0.2.1`, so `grimoire = "1.0.0"` is not a valid current source choice.
> Scry's current `deny.toml` denies unknown Git sources; the source implementation must add an explicit allow-list entry for the Grimoire repository in the same change as the benchmark dependency.
> The source node owns the clean-checkout `cargo metadata --locked` and cargo-deny checks. It does not add Grimoire to `crates/scry` or `crates/scry-mcp`.
> When a published Grimoire package and immutable release identity exist, migration is a deliberate source and benchmark-identity change with a fresh bridge comparison.
> Implemented in the workspace benchmark member: `Cargo.lock` records the exact Git revision, `deny.toml` allows only the Grimoire repository, `cargo metadata --locked` resolves `grimoire 1.0.0`, and `cargo deny check` passes.
---
[grimoire-bridge] Compile ONNX into a Grimoire description (complete)
Translate the pinned raw ONNX graph into a validated, canonical Grimoire static description for cost analysis.
Serves: the benchmark's source of truth is an addressable graph, not a runtime occurrence table.
Absent: operation counts and cost expressions can silently drift from graph topology, tensor connections, or shape extents.
Known by: a canonical description containing addressed operation blocks, tensor ports/connections, operation-family groups, explicit shape/axis facts, graph identity, and a round-trip validation result.
Do: parse the raw ONNX protobuf; give every node a deterministic address; preserve raw operator identity; represent graph inputs/outputs and tensor fan-out structurally; attach existing Grimoire architecture/operator and shape values; generate operation groups; fail on missing identity, unsupported shape, unknown source schema, or dropped node; keep raw graph, Grimoire validation, and cost projection as separate boundaries.
Accept: the generated description parses, validates, serializes canonically, reproduces the pinned structural fixture, and supplies the addressed elements and explicit axes needed by Grimoire CostModel without loading or timing the optimized runtime graph.
-> compute
-> grimoire-source
-> operations
-> workloads
> The bridge consumes the source node's exact package revision and carries that source identity into the generated artifact; source resolution is not delegated to the runtime crates or an ambient sibling checkout.
> Implemented in `crates/scry-benchmarks`: the cached model parses to 1,244 ONNX nodes and 19 operator families, generates 1,446 addressed blocks and 1,662 connections, round-trips through Grimoire canonical serialization, and evaluates the 96-member MatMul group.
> At the v1 binding `(B,S)=(1,32)`, the generated MAC report is `688,914,432` and the FMA-FLOP report is `1,377,828,864`; the latter agrees with `B * (42,467,328*S + 18,432*S^2)`.
